.Dd October 30, 2026
.Dt LEAKS 1
.Os Minkar
.Sh NAME
.Nm leaks
.Nd the memory a process allocated and can no longer reach
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nocontext
.Op Fl quiet
.Ar pid | name
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
stops a process, lists the blocks
.Xr malloc 3
gave it, and finds those it can no longer reach: no pointer to them is
left in its writable memory (its globals and stacks, outside malloc's
own), in its threads' registers, or in a block it can reach.
Those leaked; each is shown with its address, size and zone, and its
first bytes.
The process goes on when
.Nm
is done.
.Pp
The search is conservative: any word that holds an address inside a
block counts as a pointer to it, so a leak can go unseen, but a block
shown leaked has no pointer to it.
.Pp
A process is named by its pid or by part of its name.
Examining another user's process takes root.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl nocontext
Leaves out each leak's first bytes.
.It Fl quiet
Prints only the two summary lines.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when nothing leaked, 1 when something did, 2 on an error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl leaks Safari
.Dl leaks -quiet 1234
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr malloc 3
.Sh HISTORY
This
.Nm
was written for Minkar, after macOS's.
It does not show where each leak was allocated (macOS's
.Ev MallocStackLogging ) .
